Title: ZODIAC WARS: BLOODLINES
Episode: 101, "THE NEXT COUNT"
Credit: Draft 1 SHORT — PROPOSED, not approved
Author: Prepared for Ω SYD OMEGA 91717 STUDIOS GLOBAL under the founder's direction
Draft date: 2026-11-07
Notes: Cold open and three condensed acts, about 14 script pages of a 50-minute episode, plus a short tag. Far from the Nexus only Kael hears Soul (Sentinel at the pit hears nothing); inside the vault anyone present hears it, which is new in this draft. Season 1, gate INITIATE. It starts exactly where The Rise of Aries (screenplay draft 1, itself not approved) stops. Canon this draft respects: Soul's door is a hand's width open and Soul is only a hand and a voice (the film's last scene); five rings lit and four dark; the Light rulers are voices only; the foundry oath is "say the cost before the pour"; Tamsin's line "One bell. One tongue. One master we did not save." is the film's; clause nine and its reason (someone died of thirst on a world with water they were not allowed to touch) are Adaeze's words in the film. New in this draft, and the founder's to accept or strike: the Accord's schedule of clauses with a blank reason beside every clause but nine, and the idea that each clause exists because someone died. The Unbled's blood is the film's post-credit scene continued. Orla's notebook and the fourth signature are not used yet. Nothing here is filmed, released or playable.

FADE IN:

INT. THE NEXUS — NIGHT

The vault under Meridian, silent. Nine rings turn slowly in the dark: five lit, four not. The four dark rings have stopped but not lit.

At the northern quarter stands the white door, open a hand's width. Light spills through the gap, white and cold and, strangely, kind. A hand rests on the frame. It is not a man's, not a god's, something older than both. It taps the wood, slowly, one finger at a time, like someone counting.

SOUL (V.O.)
(softly, to itself)
One bell. One tongue. One master we did not save.

The finger stops.

SOUL (V.O.) (CONT'D)
And twelve thousand years of peace.
(a pause)
I have not been told what it cost.

Under the vault a single low note rises and holds: exactly nine point one seven one seven.

SMASH CUT TO:

MAIN TITLES — NINE CONCENTRIC RINGS DRAW THEMSELVES IN LIGHT, FIVE BRIGHT AND FOUR DARK. THE NOTE UNDER THEM IS 9.1717 Hz.

ACT ONE

EXT. THE ARES FOUNDRY, OLYMPUS RIDGE — MARS — NIGHT

SUPER: "THREE WEEKS AFTER THE BELL."

The Ridge, quiet under a hard sky. Some buildings are rebuilt, some still scarred. In the yard hangs the new Aries bell, its lip engraved with a single name. The furnaces are banked low. Nothing is moving.

Nothing, except one figure crossing the yard in the dark in a work coat too big at the shoulders: KAEL MARREN, nineteen, bare-headed, walking like a man who has done this walk too many times. He goes past the bell without looking at it.

INT. THE ARES FOUNDRY — THE CASTING PIT — NIGHT

An old pit of black sand and bronze scrap, lit by a single bar of furnace light. Kael stands at its rim. He does not do anything. He listens.

KAEL
(very quietly)
Not tonight.

Nothing answers. Then, from the dark behind him:

TAMSIN (O.S.)
You said that last night.

He turns. TAMSIN OKORO, a foundry apprentice, arms folded in a blanket, hair flattened on one side. Behind her, in the doorway with a lantern, BRANNOCK, a broad journeyman who has clearly been woken against his will.

KAEL
I was trying not to wake you.

BRANNOCK
You walk like a bell with a cracked lip. The whole bunk hears it.

TAMSIN
Where is it you walk to?

KAEL
(a beat; honest)
Here.

TAMSIN
It's a pit.

KAEL
It's where she stood. The last place I saw her stand.

Tamsin lowers her eyes. Brannock sets the lantern on the rim and it throws their three shadows across the black sand.

TAMSIN
(gently)
Kael. You rang the bell. You said her name to a whole plaza. You counted.

KAEL
I know.

TAMSIN
Then what is it you're still doing?

Kael looks at the pit. Then, as if it costs him to say it:

KAEL
There's a door open.

BRANNOCK
(a flat, wary look at the walls)
There's no door, lad.

KAEL
Not here. Under Earth. I can hear it from here.

A silence. Neither of them laughs.

SENTINEL (V.O.)
(from every wall, calm)
The perimeter holds. I detect nothing at the pit, Kael.

KAEL
I know you don't.

SENTINEL (V.O.)
Then I do not understand your concern.

KAEL
(a ghost of a smile)
Neither do I.

He looks at the two of them: the girl in her blanket, the man with his lantern, both of them awake at the hour of the night when nobody should be.

KAEL (CONT'D)
It said "count the next". I thought it meant I was done with one thing and it was telling me the second. I think it means I never finished the first.

TAMSIN
(a long beat)
Say the cost.

KAEL
(quietly)
If I go to Earth, the Ridge loses its Knight for however long I'm there. Tamsin pours. Brannock keeps the book. The bell stays unrung.

BRANNOCK
It'd be nice if the Knight left a note.

KAEL
(an honest wince)
I'll write.

TAMSIN
You said that last time.

KAEL
I'll write something in it.

ACT TWO

INT. THE BALANCE COURT — MERIDIAN CITY — MORNING

The Hall of the Seats: a vast circular hall under a glass dome, twelve great stone chairs in a ring, each carved with the sign of a House. In the middle of the ring, where the floor was bare for twelve thousand years, the plain pale thirteenth chair stands, no longer in a column of light. Morning, ordinary and civic. A cleaner pushes a mop between two chairs. High on the wall, the Accord bell hangs silent in its frame.

At a long table set at the edge of the ring sits ADAEZE OKONKWO-REYES, thirty-four, in a mediator's grey, reading glasses on her forehead, a pencil behind her ear and a brass hand-bell at her elbow. She is surrounded by paper, and she is not annoyed by it.

Kael comes in with his pack. She does not look up.

ADAEZE
Your ship sat outside the Court for twenty minutes with its running lights on. I assumed it was waiting for permission.

KAEL
It was.

ADAEZE
(looking up; dry)
It's the only thing from Mars that has ever asked.

KAEL
Adaeze. The Accord. Is there a list of what it cost?

She takes the glasses off her forehead and turns them over in her fingers.

ADAEZE
(slowly)
That's not a small question.

KAEL
I know.

ADAEZE
It's also not a question I've heard asked in seven years on this bench.

She stands and crosses to a pale cabinet set into the wall. She takes a key from a cord at her neck. The cabinet opens on a single large volume bound in grey cloth, its spine unlettered.

ADAEZE (CONT'D)
The schedule. Every clause of the Accord, as it was signed. It has never left this Hall.

She lays it on the table. Opens it. The pages are thick and old, ruled into three columns: CLAUSE, TEXT, REASON. The first two columns are dense with fine hand. She turns to one page, puts her finger on a line.

ADAEZE (CONT'D)
Clause nine. You've heard it.

KAEL
"Water bound to one House may be drawn by another only with the consent of the one who holds it."

ADAEZE
And this is the reason.

In the third column, in a different ink, brown and a little faded, a single line.

INSERT — THE REASON COLUMN: "A person died of thirst on a world that had water, which they were not permitted to touch."

Kael reads it twice.

KAEL
Where are the others?

ADAEZE
(carefully)
What others?

KAEL
The reasons. For the clauses.

She turns the page. The third column is empty. She turns another. Empty. Another. A hundred pages of text with nothing beside them.

ADAEZE
(quietly; she has known this for years and has not looked at it)
They were meant to be written when each clause was signed. We have this one, and I have always taken it as the exception. I thought the rest were lost to time.

KAEL
(very quietly)
Or never written.

ADAEZE
(a pause)
Say what you mean.

KAEL
Nobody was ever told to say what it cost. They signed under a number. And the number went to... wherever numbers go.

ADAEZE
(carefully, a mediator, not a believer)
That's a leap, Kael.

KAEL
It's what it said.

ADAEZE
What said?

He says nothing. She watches his face, and she understands, without hearing anything, that he is listening to something under the floor.

ADAEZE (CONT'D)
(a long breath)
Then we should go and listen to it properly.

ACT THREE

INT. THE NEXUS STAIR — DAY

The long spiral of pale stone, descending far further than any building above could hold. The walls are grained like the inside of a shell, humming. Kael goes down first. Adaeze follows with the grey volume held against her chest like a child. It has not left the Hall in twelve thousand years, and she is saying so, softly, to nobody.

ADAEZE
I'll say the cost later.

INT. THE NEXUS — DAY

The vault, silent. Five rings turning, four stopped. At the northern quarter, the white door stands open a hand's width.

Kael and Adaeze stop at the foot of the stair. The hum is a single steady tone.

KAEL
(to the door)
I came.

A pause. Then, from the gap, very softly, with all the patience of a thousand deaths:

SOUL (V.O.)
Kael Marren.

Adaeze flinches, and then does not. She heard it. She does not say so.

ADAEZE
(almost inaudible)
Oh.

SOUL (V.O.)
You counted well. A cost of forty thousand, said aloud, and one master, said aloud. It was a good beginning.

KAEL
You said count the next.

SOUL (V.O.)
Yes.
(the hand taps the frame once)
What do you think I am?

KAEL
(carefully)
Something older than the Houses.

SOUL (V.O.)
What is carried across a death. Whatever of a person goes on after the person: a name, a debt, a promise, a way of holding a spoon. It is carried by someone, or it is carried by no one.

ADAEZE
(her voice small)
And if it is carried by no one?

SOUL (V.O.)
It goes to the quiet.

KAEL
The Null.

SOUL (V.O.)
It does not like that word. It says it remembers. It remembers everything that nobody did.

Adaeze looks down at the volume in her arms, at the grey cloth, at her own hands.

ADAEZE
(slowly)
The Accord.

SOUL (V.O.)
Twelve thousand years ago the Twelve counted the cost of the war as a single number and signed beneath it. The number was large. They agreed not to look at it.

KAEL
Each clause.

SOUL (V.O.)
Each clause is a grave. Only one has been marked.

A silence. The hum holds.

ADAEZE
(very quietly, to Kael)
Clause nine.

KAEL
(to the door)
How many?

SOUL (V.O.)
Count.

KAEL
(helplessly)
I don't know how.

SOUL (V.O.)
No. You learned the first half. Say the cost, and be standing beside it. The second half is harder. Someone must carry what you have said.

Adaeze opens the volume on the stone floor at the foot of the white door and kneels beside it. She takes the pencil from behind her ear. She looks at the first clause of the Accord, and the empty column beside it.

ADAEZE
(hardly aloud)
What do I write?

SOUL (V.O.)
Whatever is true. Begin with that.

The pencil hovers.

And from somewhere above the book, from the air itself, a shimmer: a tall, lean outline draped in ember, stooped and standing very still, watching. The UNBLED. It extends one arm toward the page, as if to read.

Something falls from the arm. A drop of red lands in the empty margin beside clause one. It is slow, exact, and it sinks into the paper like ink.

Adaeze stops. Kael goes very still. The outline above them trembles. Then it is gone.

SOUL (V.O.) (CONT'D)
(gently)
That is the first one. Carry it.

CUT TO BLACK.

TAG

INT. THE ARES FOUNDRY — THE CASTING PIT — NIGHT

The pit, as before, with a bar of furnace light. Tamsin crouches at the old dark stain on the floor where Orla died, a wet cloth in her hand. She wipes. She sits back.

BRANNOCK
(from the doorway)
Fifth night.

TAMSIN
It doesn't come off.

On the old stain, in the lantern light, a fresh bead of red. Then another beside it.

BRANNOCK
(quietly)
There's two now.

TAMSIN
There were none this morning.

They look at each other. High above them, in the yard, the Aries bell hangs very still, and for a moment, with nobody touching it, it hums.

CLOSING SEQUENCE — THE NINE RINGS FADE. A SINGLE LOW TONE, 9.1717 Hz, HOLDS UNDER THE CREDITS.

THE END OF EPISODE 101 (DRAFT 1 SHORT)
